# FortiGate inventory

## Gate

Use when the user asks to list FortiGate devices, check health/status,
discover VDOMs, or inspect interfaces. Skip bare "firewall" with no
FortiGate surface. Always load `fortigate-safety` when a mutate might
follow.

Never invent a `device_id`, interface name, or VDOM. Resolve from MCP
listing this turn or from an id the user pasted.

## Prefer MCP

When a FortiGate MCP is ready (`user-mcp_fortigate` in Cursor), use these
tools — do not invent a side CLI or raw REST client while MCP works:

| Need | Tool | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| List registered devices | `list_devices` | no args |
| Device system status | `get_device_status` | requires `device_id` |
| Test one device | `test_device_connection` | requires `device_id` |
| Server health | `health` | MCP process health |
| Server connectivity | `test_connection` | FortiGate MCP connectivity |
| Discover VDOMs | `discover_vdoms` | requires `device_id` |
| List interfaces | `list_interfaces` | `device_id`, optional `vdom` |
| Interface detail | `get_interface_status` | `device_id` + `interface_name` |
| Tool schemas | `get_schema_info` | discover params when unsure |

## Discover first

1. Prefer exact `device_id` the user named.
2. Else `list_devices` — pick by id/host the user recognizes.
3. If several devices match, list ids + hosts and ask once; do not guess
   production.
4. Call `discover_vdoms` before assuming `root` when the ask spans
   multi-VDOM or the user named a non-root VDOM.
5. Prefer `list_interfaces` before inventing interface names used in
   policy or VIP craft.

## Status and health

1. `get_device_status` for firmware/uptime/system summary on a resolved
   device.
2. `test_device_connection` when reachability is in doubt.
3. `health` / `test_connection` when the MCP itself may be down — report
   that clearly instead of inventing device state.
4. Summarize: device id, reachability, notable version/status fields.
   Do not paste credentials or API tokens from tool output.

## Report shape

- Device id (+ host when known)
- VDOM when scoped
- Short status/finding (up/down, interface admin/oper state)
- One recommended next check (policies, routes, or a gated mutate)

## Out of scope

- Neighbor NGFW / cloud firewall products
- Site overlay hostnames or management IPs — resolve from MCP/`list_devices`
  or user paste, never invent
- Policy / object / route mutates (see those rules + `fortigate-safety`)
